<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">

    <!-- WHAT THIS MODULE ASKS FOR, AND THE ONE THING IT DELIBERATELY DOES NOT.

         Utilities replaces pieces of the operating system rather than providing a service, so it is
         the module in this suite with the widest *platform* surface — an input method the system
         binds, and four components that make the app eligible to be the phone's messenger. That is
         a lot of exported machinery, and the argument for each piece is below. What makes the whole
         thing defensible is the line that is missing:

           **NO INTERNET.** This module declares no network permission and has no HTTP client on its
           classpath. It is not a switch, not a setting, and not a promise about what the code does
           with a connection — there is no connection. That matters more here than anywhere else in
           the suite, because a keyboard sees every password and every message typed on the phone
           and a messenger sees every conversation, and the entire reason somebody would replace the
           two that ship with a handset is that those *do* have a route off the device.

           The word list the keyboard learns from is a text file in this app's own directory, capped
           at a few thousand words, readable on the keyboard's settings screen, and carried only by
           the suite's own archive.

         THE PERMISSIONS

           1. SMS — READ_SMS, RECEIVE_SMS, SEND_SMS, RECEIVE_MMS, RECEIVE_WAP_PUSH. The first three
              are the *reading rung* of the Messages takeover: drawing the threads that are already
              on the phone, and replying to them, while the carrier's app keeps delivering. All of
              them are runtime permissions and every one can be declined; declining costs exactly
              the feature it belongs to and nothing else. The last two are only ever exercised while
              this app holds the default-SMS role.

           2. READ_CONTACTS — so a thread says "Ada" rather than "(555) 010-9999". Optional in the
              strongest sense: declining it is handled everywhere, and the shelf says plainly that
              threads will be titled by number. It is read-only and never written back.

           3. POST_NOTIFICATIONS — declared by the container for the whole package; this module
              posts on the messages channel only while it is the default messenger, because a second
              notification for every text the carrier's app already announced would be the most
              obvious way to make the phone worse.

         THE EXPORTED COMPONENTS — five, in two groups.

           The KEYBOARD service is exported behind `BIND_INPUT_METHOD`, a permission held by the
           platform and by nothing installable. "Exported" there does not mean reachable by another
           app; it means the operating system can bind it, which is the only way an input method can
           exist. It is inert until the household switches it on in system settings *and* selects
           it, which are two separate deliberate acts Android makes them perform, and it can be
           handed back with a long press on the space bar.

           The FOUR MESSAGING COMPONENTS are the set Android requires before it will offer an app in
           the default-SMS role picker: a receiver for delivered texts (`BROADCAST_SMS`, held by the
           platform), a receiver for the WAP push that carries picture messages (`BROADCAST_WAP_PUSH`,
           likewise), a service the call screen can reply through (`SEND_RESPOND_VIA_MESSAGE`,
           likewise), and an activity that answers `sms:` links. The first three are reachable only
           by the system. The fourth is reachable by any app, and is therefore deliberately the
           narrowest thing in this module: `SendToActivity` has no layout, returns no result, reads
           a number out of a URI and starts the real screen. An app that launches it learns nothing —
           not whether the number is known, not whether a thread exists, not what is in one. It does
           not even carry the `?body=` across, because a link that pre-fills a message is one
           mistaken tap from sending somebody else's words from your number.

           MainActivity itself is NOT exported, like every other hosted app's screen. The sandbox
           opens it in-process.

         WHAT IS NOT WRITTEN, SAID HERE AS WELL AS IN THE APP

           MMS. Becoming the default messenger means Android stops handing picture messages to the
           carrier's app and starts handing them here, and the MMS half of this module does not
           exist: text arrives and is stored, a picture message arrives as a notification this app
           records and cannot fetch. The app says so in a dialog before the role picker opens (see
           `Takeovers.mmsWarning`), the receiver says so in its own comment, and switching the role
           back restores it immediately — nothing is moved or deleted by the takeover. -->

    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.READ_SMS" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.RECEIVE_SMS" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.SEND_SMS" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.RECEIVE_MMS" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.RECEIVE_WAP_PUSH" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.READ_CONTACTS" />

    <!-- A tablet with no radio can still use the keyboard, so the host app must not inherit a hard
         requirement from half of this module. -->
    <uses-feature
        android:name="android.hardware.telephony"
        android:required="false" />

    <!-- Utilities is a library module: no app-level attributes here (name/icon/theme/label live on
         the :app host). Only this app's own components are declared for the manifest merger. -->
    <application>

        <!-- Not a launcher, and not exported: the Operations Sandbox home opens this explicitly,
             in-process. `singleTask` so that a notification or an `sms:` link arriving while the app
             is already open reuses the screen instead of stacking a second copy behind it. -->
        <activity
            android:name=".MainActivity"
            android:exported="false"
            android:launchMode="singleTask"
            android:windowSoftInputMode="adjustResize" />

        <!-- ================= The keyboard ================= -->

        <service
            android:name=".keyboard.UtilitiesKeyboardService"
            android:label="@string/utilities_keyboard_name"
            android:exported="true"
            android:permission="android.permission.BIND_INPUT_METHOD">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.view.InputMethod" />
            </intent-filter>
            <meta-data
                android:name="android.view.im"
                android:resource="@xml/utilities_keyboard" />
        </service>

        <!-- ================= Messages ================= -->

        <!-- Delivered texts. Sent to exactly one app — the default one — so if this receiver does
             not write the message down, no app on the phone has it. See SmsDeliverReceiver. -->
        <receiver
            android:name=".messages.SmsDeliverReceiver"
            android:exported="true"
            android:permission="android.permission.BROADCAST_SMS">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.provider.Telephony.SMS_DELIVER" />
            </intent-filter>
        </receiver>

        <!-- The WAP push that announces a picture message. Declared because the role requires it;
             what it can actually do is the honest part — see MmsDeliverReceiver. -->
        <receiver
            android:name=".messages.MmsDeliverReceiver"
            android:exported="true"
            android:permission="android.permission.BROADCAST_WAP_PUSH">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.provider.Telephony.WAP_PUSH_DELIVER" />
                <data android:mimeType="application/vnd.wap.mms-message" />
            </intent-filter>
        </receiver>

        <!-- What the radio says about a text this app handed it. Not exported: the only thing that
             can reach it is the PendingIntent this app made, which names this package. -->
        <receiver
            android:name=".messages.SmsSentReceiver"
            android:exported="false">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="com.utilities.app.messages.SMS_SENT" />
            </intent-filter>
        </receiver>

        <!-- "Reply with a message", from the call screen. Binds to nothing and shows nothing. -->
        <service
            android:name=".messages.RespondViaMessageService"
            android:exported="true"
            android:permission="android.permission.SEND_RESPOND_VIA_MESSAGE">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.RESPOND_VIA_MESSAGE" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.DEFAULT" />
                <data android:scheme="sms" />
                <data android:scheme="smsto" />
                <data android:scheme="mms" />
                <data android:scheme="mmsto" />
            </intent-filter>
        </service>

        <!-- The one component in this module any app can reach. See the block at the top for the
             five properties that keep it narrow. `excludeFromRecents` because it is an errand inside
             somebody else's task rather than a place to come back to. -->
        <activity
            android:name=".messages.SendToActivity"
            android:exported="true"
            android:excludeFromRecents="true"
            android:noHistory="true"
            android:stateNotNeeded="true"
            android:theme="@android:style/Theme.NoDisplay">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.SENDTO" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.DEFAULT" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.BROWSABLE" />
                <data android:scheme="sms" />
                <data android:scheme="smsto" />
                <data android:scheme="mms" />
                <data android:scheme="mmsto" />
            </intent-filter>
        </activity>

    </application>

</manifest>
